<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../Graphics/Australia_20050921.jpg"><img class="PicBorder" src="Graphics/AustraliaSmall_20050921.jpg" align="right"></a>
<u>08-SEP-2005 8:43 PM (AUS Time)</u><br>
We had only a short list of things to see today on the Gold Coast, but the the trip would take some time as there would be a lot of backtracking due to the roads that we needed to go on, not being connected.  Lamington National Park and Springbrook National Park, Mt. Tambourine were all on seperated roads.
<p>
We started our journey through Mt. Tambourine in the foothills of the mountains.  Mt. Tambourine was a collection of art galleries, cafes, and coffee shops, similar to Cannon Beach.  It wasn't really for us as we were looking for more natural attractions.
<p>
That's where the national parks came in.  However, we were hopelessly lost and pulled over to ask a gas station attendent for directions to Lamington NP.  The problem so far that I've seen while driving around here is the lack of road signs with directions.  You really only know you are going the right direction when you reach a road as the same name as your destination for example Lamington Rd.  Without a fairly detailed map we were using common sense and my great directional know-how (positioning of the sun, gut feeling, whatever other magic).  
<p>
After finding our proper road it become a nice winding road through the hills and mountains.  At some point the road would narrow down to one lane and then back to two.  This process repeated over and over again.
<p>
Just before I reached Lamington NP, a wallaby jumped off into the bushes, making it my first sighting.
<p>
The first spot was called O'Reilly's which included an amazing walk among the treetops and a climb to the top of a tree for a bird's eye view. During this time we had our second encounter with a wallaby.
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439556_1.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439556_1.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126439556_1.jpg"></a>
<p>
Backtracking to the 2nd part of the park, M and I hiked down to the caves where Aborigines used to cook at.  
<p>
The next park we were going to was called Springbrook NP.  One of the biggest attractions of Springbrook NP is called Natural Bridge.  Just like it sounds, it is a bridge that formed naturally as a result of a river that carved a hole into the roof of a cave. Pretty cool, huh?  On the way down I came across a python coiled up. I didn't even seem him at first until some lady pointed him out to me first.  Leaving the snake to his own business, I walked down to the bottom of this natural bridge and inside the cave where it was dark except for a hole created by the river.  What an amazingly beautiful place.  And in the evenings after dusk, the glowworms would be visible and the bats would come out.  However, I only spotted bats and not glowworms because I didn't really have time to wait around until dusk.
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439283_4.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439283_4.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126439283_4.jpg"></a>
<p>
Fairly impressed with this attraction, and with the sun setting in about 1 hour, I needed to drive to one other location. It was a rock pool that people swam in.  Reading my guide book, it had warned of eels in the water.  Anyway, after making a series of wrong turns, I did eventually make the right turn and drove there just as the sun was setting, but there was enough light to see the rock pool. And the first thing I spotted were those eels.  Kinda scary, I wouldn't get into the water with them.  They were thin and white, almost translucent with a flagellent tail.
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439556_2.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439556_2.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126439556_2.jpg"></a>
<p>
It was time to head back to our fabulous resort hostel.  M wanted to sleep but I wanted to play, so I went out on the town to check out the nightlife.  The strip of road we were on is called the Gold Coast Highway and it was loaded with lots of hotels, apartments, and condos.  However, the most popular street located full of restuarants, clubs, and young kids in their early 20's in clubby attire was called Cavill Street.  The clubs didn't really interest me, nor did all the drunken youth, but the area was very lively with people and lights and sounds and that's what attracted me.
<p>
Having gotten a little hungry I grabbed a small snack from McDonald's and headed to the beach to sit there in the sand at night and listen to the waves crashing on the shoreline. How relaxing.
<p>
I wanted to try another Australian beer, so I headed back to a more quiet bar along the GC Hwy.  A place called The Beachhouse.  Whenever I ask for a pint of beer, most bartenders are clueless so I ask for a cup because that's what I've heard the locals say.  This time around I wanted to try a very popularly advertised Australian beer called Victoria Bitter.  I sat down with my cup of beer and enjoyed a cricket match with Australia vs England.
<p>
Well tomorrow was when we flew out and it was a morning flight so I called it a night and headed home.  
